======================================================================================================
S1. THE FRAGILITY IS REAL -- and worse than 'z < -1'. alpha=1 is bounded GLOBALLY; alpha=2 is not
======================================================================================================
  alpha=1 AT its branch point z = -1/4:  K_1 = I,  |K_1| = 1
  [OK  ] alpha=1 is FINITE at its own branch point, with |K_1| = 1 exactly -- no divergence anywhere
  alpha=2 AS z -> -1:  |K_2| * sqrt(1+z) -> 1  =>  |K_2| ~ (1+z)^(-1/2) BLOWUP
  [OK  ] alpha=2 has a genuine (1+z)^(-1/2) divergence at z = -1, with unit coefficient -- a real singularity where alpha=1 has a finite value

  ON THE CUT z = -s. This is where it is worse than I first said:
           s      |K_1(-s)|      |K_2(-s)|
        0.01   0.1010205144   0.1005037815
         0.2   0.6180339887            0.5
         0.5            1.0            1.0
        0.75            1.0    1.732050808
         0.9            1.0            3.0
        0.99            1.0    9.949874371
  [OK  ] |K_2| = 1.0 = 1 EXACTLY at s = 1/2, so boundedness fails on the ENTIRE UPPER HALF of alpha=2's own measure support (0,1) -- not merely at the far edge
  [OK  ] whereas |K_1| = 1 IDENTICALLY for s > 1/4 (unimodular on its cut), so alpha=1 saturates the bound but never exceeds it. The asymmetry is genuine and it is not a rounding artefact

