======================================================================================================
S3. *** AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL: K(z) = mu(sqrt z), so F(kappa) = mu(kappa) ***
======================================================================================================
  This is what actually closes the thread. The kernel and the interpolating function are the
  same object with a substituted argument -- check it for BOTH, symbolically:
    alpha=1:  K(x^2) - mu(x) = 0
  [OK  ] alpha=1: K(z) = mu(sqrt z) IDENTICALLY (residual 0) -- the kernel IS the interpolating function with argument sqrt(z)
    alpha=2:  K(x^2) - mu(x) = 0
  [OK  ] alpha=2: K(z) = mu(sqrt z) IDENTICALLY (residual 0) -- the kernel IS the interpolating function with argument sqrt(z)

  CONSEQUENCE, and it is the closure: the Euclidean loop symbol is F(kappa) = K(kappa^2) =
  mu(kappa). So Lane A's boundedness requirement 'F bounded in [0,1)' is EXACTLY the statement
      mu <= 1,
  which is a DEFINING property of any MOND interpolating function -- it is what makes mu an
  interpolation between the deep and Newtonian regimes at all. It is not an extra assumption
  about the measure, it is the phenomenology. *** So the boundedness input CANNOT FAIL for any
  alpha, and the z < -1 blemish is irrelevant to the loop. THREAD CLOSED. ***
    strict check at 40 dps: max mu over kappa in [1e-8, 1e8] = 0.99999999999999995
                            1 - max mu = 5.0e-17  (positive => strictly below 1)
  [OK  ] mu < 1 STRICTLY for both kernels over seventeen decades (1 - max mu = 5.0e-17 > 0), saturating unity only as kappa -> infinity. float64 rounds this to exactly 1.0 by kappa ~ 1e8, which is why the check is done in mpmath -- the bound is strict, not marginal

======================================================================================================
S4. AUDIT: did the ORIGINAL Lane A use the framework and its own numbers correctly?
======================================================================================================
  Four of its internal claims, re-derived here independently rather than taken on trust.

  (a) Lane A checks the M_(-1/2) log slope equals 1/(2 pi). Derive it: in region B,
      rho_B = 1/(2 pi sqrt t), so the integrand is t^(-1/2)/(2 pi sqrt t) = 1/(2 pi t),
      whose integral is ln(T)/(2 pi) -- slope d/d(ln T) = 1/(2 pi) exactly.
      measured slope = 0.159154943092,  1/(2 pi) = 0.159154943092
  [OK  ] Lane A's log-slope claim is CORRECT: measured 0.1591549431 vs 1/(2 pi) = 0.1591549431, agreeing to 2.87e-42 -- far tighter than the 3e-3 tolerance it used on itself

  (b) Lane A checks K(0) = 0 and K(inf) = 1 from the measure. Re-derive from ITS density:
      M_(-1) from Lane A's own two-region density = 0.999999999968
  [OK  ] Lane A's measure gives M_(-1) = 1.0 = K(inf) - K(0) = 1 -- so its stated boundary values and its density are mutually consistent. The original used its own measure correctly

  (c) alpha=1: K/sqrt(z) -> 1 at z -> 0, so K'(0) = infinity and M_(-2) diverges

  (c) alpha=2: K/sqrt(z) -> 1 at z -> 0, so K'(0) = infinity and M_(-2) diverges
  [OK  ] the deep-MOND sqrt(z) nonanalyticity that makes M_(-2) diverge is present on BOTH kernels, so Lane A's identification of it as a property of the LAW rather than of its measure was right

  (d) NOTHING FOUND WRONG in the four claims audited. Lane A's numbers are its own and they
      hold. The one thing it could not have known is that its kernel would later be retired,
      which is exactly the gap this series of files has been closing -- not an error on its part.

======================================================================================================
S5. WHAT IS *NOT* CLOSED -- the ON-SHELL side, and it must not be conflated with the loop
======================================================================================================
  The loop lives at Euclidean kappa^2 >= 0 (S2). The ON-SHELL Lorentzian side does not: there
  z = -w^2 with w = c*Omega/a0, so z is NEGATIVE, and z < -1 whenever w > 1, i.e. whenever
      Omega > a0/c.

    footing                     a0/c (1/s)      period at w=1
    canonical cH_L/Z            3.1222e-19           637.7 Gyr
    alternate rho_tot/cH0       3.7693e-19           528.2 Gyr
  [OK  ] w = 1 corresponds to a period of several hundred Gyr on both footings, so EVERY real orbit has w >> 1 and therefore sits at z << -1 -- the region where alpha=2 exceeds unity

    system                         w = c Omega/a0   K_2 - 1 = 1/(2w^2)
    wide binary 10 kAU                   9.60e+05            5.425e-13
    galaxy 10 kpc                        7.80e+02            8.218e-07
    dwarf spheroidal                     5.20e+02            1.849e-06
    cluster member                       1.00e+02            5.000e-05
  [OK  ] but the excess is 1/(2w^2) = 5e-5 at worst (cluster members) and 5e-13 for wide binaries -- real, nonzero, and numerically negligible for every physical system

  SO THE HONEST SPLIT: the LOOP thread is closed (S2, S3 -- boundedness is only ever needed at
  kappa^2 >= 0, where it is guaranteed by mu <= 1). The ON-SHELL passivity blemish is NOT closed
  by anything here -- it is real, it applies to every physical orbit, and it is Theorem 8's
  territory rather than the loop's. It is also 1e-13 to 1e-5 in size, which is why it has not
  shown up as a phenomenological problem. Conflating the two would be a mistake in either
  direction: the loop is safe, and the on-shell blemish is still on the books.

======================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================
  THE THREAD IS CLOSED, AND BY A STRUCTURAL IDENTITY RATHER THAN A BOUND CHECK.

   1. THE FRAGILITY IS REAL, and worse than I first stated. alpha=1 is bounded by unity
      EVERYWHERE -- K_1 = i exactly at its branch point z = -1/4, and |K_1| = 1 identically on
      its cut beyond. alpha=2 DIVERGES at z = -1 like (1+z)^(-1/2), and |K_2(-s)| passes unity
      at s = 1/2, so boundedness fails on the ENTIRE UPPER HALF of alpha=2's own measure
      support -- not merely at the far edge, which is what I said before.

   2. *** BUT THE LOOP NEVER GOES THERE. *** Lane A uses boundedness in exactly one place: the
      Euclidean longitudinal symbol F(kappa) = K(kappa^2) with kappa = k0/a0 REAL. The argument
      is kappa^2 >= 0, strictly the positive axis. alpha=2 passes all FOUR of Lane A's own
      criteria on F -- monotone, bounded in [0,1), F(0) = 0, and F ~ kappa at small kappa (the
      same nonanalytic |k0|/a0 behaviour) -- with a mutation control confirming the criteria
      discriminate.

   3. AND THE REASON IS AN IDENTITY: K(z) = mu(sqrt z) for BOTH kernels (sympy residual 0), so
      F(kappa) = mu(kappa). Lane A's requirement 'F bounded in [0,1)' is therefore EXACTLY
      'mu <= 1' -- a DEFINING property of any MOND interpolating function, not an extra
      assumption about the measure. *** The boundedness input cannot fail for any alpha. ***
      That is a stronger closure than checking a bound numerically: it says the loop's one
      boundedness need is supplied by the phenomenology itself.

   4. THE ORIGINAL LANE A CHECKS OUT. Four of its internal claims re-derived independently: the
      M_(-1/2) log slope is 1/(2 pi) (agreeing to 1e-6, far tighter than its own 3e-3
      tolerance); its two-region density reproduces M_(-1) = K(inf) - K(0) = 1; and its
      identification of the sqrt(z) nonanalyticity as a property of the LAW rather than the
      measure is correct, since both kernels share it. NOTHING FOUND WRONG. The only thing it
      could not have known is that its kernel would later be retired.

  *** WHAT IS STILL NOT CLOSED, and must not be conflated with the loop: *** the ON-SHELL
  Lorentzian side. There z = -w^2 is negative and z < -1 whenever Omega > a0/c, i.e. for every
  orbit with a period under ~640 Gyr -- which is every real system. The passivity excess there
  is 1/(2w^2): 5e-5 for cluster members, 5e-13 for wide binaries. Real, nonzero, negligible,
  and Theorem 8's territory rather than the loop's. The loop is safe; that blemish stays on the
  books.

  STILL OPEN: two loops; the finite parts; the T_mu_nu metric variation; the disformal rho_m
  variant; the ephemeris de/dt bound. a0 is NOT derived, kappa = 1/2 stays FITTED, the pincer is
  untouched (Theorem 3 forbids all local L, Theorem 8's argument mismatch stands), and no door
  is declared closed.
